

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Due/overdue only — that simplifies things a lot. No change log, no edit tracking; the due date [3mis[0m the event source.

  Which exposes the scope question I have to ask before designing anything: [1mthere are no tasks.[0m [38;5;153mindex.html[39m has an empty
  [38;5;153m<main>[39m. For due/overdue notifications to exist, tasks need to exist, be persisted, and carry due dates. So the work is
  at minimum a small task app plus a notifications layer on top.

  - [1mMinimal task substrate[0m — tasks with a title, done state, and optional due date; localStorage persistence;
    add/complete/delete. Just enough for notifications to be real. The notifications layer is the actual deliverable;
    the task list is scaffolding kept deliberately thin.
  - [1mNotifications only, against an assumed model[0m — I define a task interface and build the notification engine against
    it, leaving the UI a stub. You get a tested engine with nothing to look at, and someone has to wire it up later.
  - [1mFull task app[0m — editing, priorities, filtering, sorting, tags. Notifications become one feature among many, and the
    spec gets much bigger.

  Cheap to change later: the task list's look, extra fields, storage backend behind a small persistence wrapper.
  Expensive: the shape of the task record itself, since the notification engine reads it.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m the minimal substrate. It keeps the deliverable honest — you can actually see a notification fire —
  without spending the spec on task-app features you didn't ask for.
